<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tabriz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tabriz is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, historically one of the principal commercial and political centres of the region. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> it became a major hub of trade where Mongol, Persian, Armenian and European merchants met. In 1501 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> captured Tabriz and proclaimed himself Shah, making it the first capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, though it was later repeatedly contested with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tabriz.jpg" alt="Tabriz" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elmju · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tabriz_Municipality_Sa%27at_Tower.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tabriz lies in the valley of the Quru River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> region, between the Sahand and Eynali mountains, at an elevation of between 1,350 and 1,600 metres. The valley opens into a plain that slopes gently down to the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-urmia">Lake Urmia</a>, 60 kilometres to the west. The area is prone to earthquakes, and the city has been devastated and rebuilt several times; earthquakes are recorded in 858, 1041 and 1721. According to some sources the name derives from tap-riz, meaning flowing hot, in reference to the area's thermal springs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Early history is poorly documented. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> graveyard of the first millennium BCE was found beside the Blue Mosque, and the city appears as Tarui or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauri">Tauris</a> in an inscription of the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> dated 714 BCE. After the Muslim conquest the Azd tribe from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> settled there. In 791 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zubaidah-bint-jafar">Zubaidah</a>, wife of the Abbasid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a>, rebuilt the city after a devastating earthquake and beautified it so much that she was credited as its founder.</p><p>Tabriz was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> from 1265, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazan-khan">Ghazan Khan</a>, who came to power in 1295, it reached its highest splendour. It was again a capital under the Qara Qoyunlu from 1375 to 1468 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> from 1468 to 1500. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> was crowned there in 1501 and announced Twelver Shia Islam as the official religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, and the largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> population was forced to convert. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran">Battle of Chaldiran</a> in 1514 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> sacked the city, and in 1555 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Tahmasp I</a> moved the capital to Qazvin to escape the growing Ottoman threat.</p><p>Its closeness to the West made Tabriz the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution">Iranian Constitutional Revolution</a> between 1905 and 1911, led locally by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sattar-khan">Sattar Khan</a> and Bagher Khan. Russian forces occupied it in 1909 and again from December 1911, executing about 1,200 residents, and held it until 1917.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tabriz is the capital of East Azerbaijan province and the largest economic centre of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, with a population of over 1.7 million in 2016. Most of its people speak Iranian Azerbaijani as their first language and Persian as their second. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">Bazaar</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, and most preserved monuments date from the Ilkhanid, Safavid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> periods. The city is known for hand-woven carpets, and the University of Tabriz was established in 1947.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tabriz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The confederation also controlled important stretches of the caravan routes that carried Iranian raw silk westwards to Bursa and Aleppo, and <b>Tabriz</b>, its capital after 1469, was one of the great commercial cities of western Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Mongols made <b>Tabriz</b> the capital of the Ilkhanate in the second half of the thirteenth century, Trebizond became a principal Black Sea outlet for the route from Persia, through which silk, spices and other goods reached Western merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hulagu made Maragha in Azerbaijan his capital, later supplanted by <b>Tabriz</b>, and adopted the title “Ilkhan”, usually understood as a subordinate khan acknowledging the supremacy of the Great Khan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Constitutionalists held out in <b>Tabriz</b> under leaders such as Sattar Khan, and in July 1909 forces from Gilan and the Bakhtiari tribes took Tehran and deposed the shah in favour of his young son Ahmad Shah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the fourteenth century they lived in the shadow of the Jalayirid Sultanate of Baghdad and <b>Tabriz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He led the Qizilbash, a confederation of Turkmen tribes (traditionally seven) named for their distinctive red headgear, to victory over the Aq Qoyunlu at the Battle of Sharur, seized <b>Tabriz</b> and proclaimed himself Shah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
